# TagSession index validation

Index-based `TagSession` APIs must not silently no-op on out-of-range indices. UI code can hold stale row indices after `removeTag`, import, or filter rebuilds.

- **`isValidTagIndex(int)`** — single bounds check shared by session and callers.
- **Mutators** (`removeTag`, `setTagNote`, `setTagInterval`) return **`bool`**: `false` on invalid index, `true` on success or idempotent no-op (unchanged note/interval).
- **`Q_ASSERT_X`** on invalid index in debug builds — catches programmer errors; release builds still return `false` without crashing.
- **`tagNote`** returns empty `QString()` on invalid index (same assert). Callers that must distinguish an empty note from a miss must call `isValidTagIndex` first.
- Resolve identity with **`indexOfTagId`** before using a cached index; do not assume table row order equals session index after mutations.

```cpp
// BAD — void return; caller cannot tell if delete/note/trim applied
void removeTag(int index) {
  if (index < 0 || index >= tags_.size()) return;
  tags_.removeAt(index);
}

// GOOD — explicit miss handling at the call site
const int sessionIndex = tagSession_->indexOfTagId(tagId);
if (!tagSession_->isValidTagIndex(sessionIndex)) return;
if (!tagSession_->setTagNote(sessionIndex, noteText)) return;
```

Do not emit change signals when validation fails — that desynchronizes UI listeners.
